@doc ST-STANDARDS [ref, active, agents+employees] v1

@rules
  1. CommonJS module system. All files use 'use strict' and require/module.exports. No ESM in production code.
  2. Node.js >= 18.0.0 required. Use node: protocol for built-in imports (node:fs, node:path, node:crypto).
  3. Zero third-party runtime dependencies. All functionality is implemented with Node.js standard library only.
  4. Tests use node:test (the built-in test runner). Run with npm test at the package level or root level.
  5. Each package is independently versioned, tested, and documented. Root npm scripts delegate to workspace packages.
  6. CLI tools expose a bin entry in package.json. The binary is a thin wrapper that calls the main function from src/cli/main.js.
  7. Every conversion must produce provenance and drop reports alongside the SIGN output. No silent data loss.
  8. Semantic completeness is checked after emission. Gaps are surfaced as warnings by default; --strict-semantic makes high-severity gaps fail the run.
  9. The emitter is deterministic. Given the same input and ontology, it produces identical output including the sha: hash.
  10. Configuration files (doctype maps, ontologies, typemaps) are loaded from the filesystem via explicit paths. No environment-variable-based config.
  11. Error handling uses thrown Error objects with an exitCode property for CLI-visible failures.
  12. Functions are small and single-purpose. The pipeline is composed of pure-ish functions connected in convertDocument.
  13. Skills (sign-draft) are pure markdown definitions. They carry no runtime code; the LLM interprets the skill definition at invocation time.
  14. Synced references must not be edited in place. Edit the canonical source in sign-lang, then run the sync script.
  15. New tools go in packages/<tool-name>/ with their own README, SPEC (if applicable), package.json, and tests.

@disallowed
  + no-esm-in-production     | Do not use ES module syntax (import/export) in production source files. CommonJS only.
  + no-third-party-deps      | Do not add third-party npm runtime dependencies. Dev dependencies for testing or tooling are acceptable if justified.
  + no-cross-package-imports  | Do not import from one package into another at runtime. Shared code goes to shared/ with an explicit contract.
  + no-internal-sign-parsing | Do not parse or validate SIGN internally. Shell out to the sign-lang validator.
  + no-silent-drops          | Do not discard source content without recording it in the drop report.
  + no-manual-spec-edits     | Do not edit the synced spec reference in sign-draft. Use the sync script.
  + no-global-state          | Do not use module-level mutable state. Functions receive their dependencies as arguments.
  + no-database-layers       | Do not add database access, ORMs, or persistence beyond filesystem reads and writes.

@constraints
  MIT license applies to all code. Contributions must be compatible.
  The agentic operating system (.agent/) governs development workflow. Plans, decisions, and policies live there.
  Approval boundaries defined in .agent/policies/approvals.md must be respected. Stop and request human approval for destructive, auth, or infrastructure changes.
  The SIGN spec (sign-lang) is the upstream authority. sign-tools implements the spec; it does not extend or modify it.
  Coding standards in .agent/policies/agent-coding-standards.md apply to all code in this repo.
